o this is the slide from earlier we  want to focus  on the white rectangles here we are  importing  torches ladybug metrics here and we are  printing the metrics report  this metrics report contains a lot of  information about what's going on at the  xla level  and it can surface some of the issues  that i just discussed in  in the previous slides and let's look at  an examples metrics report this is  just a summary that focuses on the  issues that we discussed  the actual metrics report contains more  information than just this  if you look at the first part the the  metric compile time this gives us  information about how many graph  recompilations have occurred in this  workload  and in this case we have 202  compilations that occurred and the total  time it took  is six minutes now the metric support is  usually uh  composed of metrics and the counters the  counters  give information about what's happening  in the code  for example the counter mark step uh  tells you that  uh how many training steps  that we have uh we have taken for  example in this case  232 steps have been taken and that  resulted in 202 compilations  which suggests that there's a problem  with uh  the the uh graphs that the code is  generating the compilations should be  uh at low some compilations initially  are okay  but it shouldn't grow as the number of  steps grow  similarly for example in the item call  problem the context switch between  device and cpu the counter atom local  scalar dance will be  will be your friend and here in this  case you have 240  occurrences of that and that's uh  that's something that needs to be  reduced to get optimal performance  in the third problem that we discussed  the op that's not been lowered yet  you will see an a10 counter with the ops  actual name  and in this case uh we have 232  probably once per step that  some op that needs to be lower in xla  and once you  iron those issues out the pro the code  will run much faster and will be very  performant  uh now so far we have seen how training  works on a cloud tp device  now let's discuss how to take it to the  next level  um and train on a cloud tp pod  as uh pods were described by vibhav  earlier  they consist of several devices  connected with very fast network and in  this  in the pytorch xla context we create  uh as many user vms as the cloud tp  devices  not cores so for example if you want to  train on a v3 332 32 course we would  need to create four vms  to to drive the training and we wrap  these user vms in an instance group so  everything needs to be in an instance  group and once we have set up these  resources  launching the training distributed mode  is very easy  now let's look at the command to do that  here the white  part of the large command is the actual  command that you were running to run on  one device so this is your code  with arguments and all you need to do to  scale it up to a pod level is is just  prefix the command with with these  elements  and here the green part is xladist  it's offered by torch xla and it  coordinates the resources  talks sets up the mesh between the  instance group and the tpus  and sets up the environment variables  specified by the next uh  set of elements uh for example the tpu  name the conda environment and any other  environment  variable that you might want to set up  and that's it  um it's there's really no code changes  uh  one gotcha here is uh the code needs to  be written  so that the data is sharded across all  the cores  uh otherwise um the  vms will end up doing the same work  unnecessary work  but that that's really the only gotcha  and scaling up is  is as easy as just prefixing your  command  so now let's look at some of the models  um that actually  have been tested and known to work uh  for in the computer vision uh area uh we  have resnet and resnecks and along with  most storage vision models such as  mobilenet and others  uh that already work in the nlp area we  have  transformers network uh for example fair  seeks transformer roberta  or hugging faces transformers we have a  working implementation of a deep  convolutional  again that's up on our github  and in the speech recognition world we  have  worked with uh fair six wave to wek and  vector quantized  vape tobacco let me note here that the  models that work on torch xl aren't  limited to these  these have been tested but  people have run other workloads and  other models  successfully as well and you can import  your model as well  to get started here are some resources  so  these are some of the tutorials that are  offered offered on google cloud web  pages  and it covers some of the models that i  just discussed in a previous slide  and along with the google cloud  tutorials there are  kaggle kernels from some of the kaggle  experts in the community  that they have successfully run uh  competitions on torch xla and they offer  their kernels  to the users and finally here's a  summary slide  of all the resources that we saw um  some of the github repositories that are  relevant to the project official  tutorials kaggle kernels  and please visit pytorch xla it's a very  active  open source library and  open issues if you encounter any issues  etc  thanks for listening
 

